1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,054
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,234 --> 00:00:04,771
Walk through any parking lot in America

3
00:00:04,771 --> 00:00:07,243
and you'll see thousands of beautiful,

4
00:00:07,243 --> 00:00:07,958
shiny cars.

5
00:00:08,138 --> 00:00:09,485
Here's what you won't see.

6
00:00:09,665 --> 00:00:11,116
The loan behind each one.

7
00:00:11,296 --> 00:00:13,887
Americans now owe around one point six

8
00:00:13,887 --> 00:00:16,001
trillion dollars on auto loans,

9
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:18,320
and the average new car payment is

10
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:20,774
higher than many people's rent was a

11
00:00:20,774 --> 00:00:21,525
decade ago.

12
00:00:21,705 --> 00:00:24,195
Today, we're talking about the car payment

13
00:00:24,195 --> 00:00:24,491
trap.

14
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:27,198
Auto loan balances in the United States

15
00:00:27,198 --> 00:00:29,738
are around one point six trillion dollars,

16
00:00:29,738 --> 00:00:32,156
according to the Federal Reserve Bank of

17
00:00:32,156 --> 00:00:32,700
New York.

18
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:35,223
That's more than Americans owe on credit

19
00:00:35,223 --> 00:00:35,574
cards.

20
00:00:35,923 --> 00:00:38,454
Cars are necessary for most Americans.

21
00:00:38,634 --> 00:00:40,580
You need one to get to work,

22
00:00:40,580 --> 00:00:41,970
take kids to school,

23
00:00:41,970 --> 00:00:43,220
and buy groceries.

24
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:45,795
That's exactly why the car trap is

25
00:00:45,795 --> 00:00:46,640
so powerful.

26
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:49,606
It hides inside something you genuinely need.

27
00:00:49,786 --> 00:00:51,588
And the gap between the car you

28
00:00:51,588 --> 00:00:53,449
need and the car you're sold can

29
00:00:53,449 --> 00:00:55,833
quietly cost you hundreds of thousands of

30
00:00:55,833 --> 00:00:57,228
dollars over a lifetime.

31
00:00:57,578 --> 00:00:59,807
We'll look at it from three angles.

32
00:00:59,987 --> 00:01:01,358
First, the numbers:

33
00:01:01,358 --> 00:01:02,946
what cars really cost,

34
00:01:02,946 --> 00:01:04,317
beyond the sticker.

35
00:01:04,497 --> 00:01:06,045
Second, the psychology:

36
00:01:06,045 --> 00:01:08,400
how dealers and our own brains turn

37
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:10,890
a sensible purchase into a seven year

38
00:01:10,890 --> 00:01:11,428
payment.

39
00:01:11,608 --> 00:01:13,290
And third, the playbook:

40
00:01:13,290 --> 00:01:14,060
how to buy,

41
00:01:14,060 --> 00:01:16,162
finance and keep a car the way

42
00:01:16,162 --> 00:01:17,843
wealthy people often do.

43
00:01:18,193 --> 00:01:19,745
And stay for the part about the

44
00:01:19,745 --> 00:01:21,548
hundred thousand dollar car payment.

45
00:01:21,728 --> 00:01:23,419
It's not a car that costs one

46
00:01:23,419 --> 00:01:24,410
hundred thousand.

47
00:01:24,590 --> 00:01:26,343
It's a much more ordinary story.

48
00:01:26,523 --> 00:01:28,113
Let's start with the numbers.

49
00:01:28,464 --> 00:01:30,592
Every number here comes from a named

50
00:01:30,592 --> 00:01:31,006
source.

51
00:01:31,186 --> 00:01:33,441
Auto debt totals come from the Federal

52
00:01:33,441 --> 00:01:34,925
Reserve Bank of New York.

53
00:01:35,105 --> 00:01:38,249
Typical payments, prices and loan terms come

54
00:01:38,249 --> 00:01:41,250
from industry research firms like Edmunds,

55
00:01:41,250 --> 00:01:43,394
Experian and Kelley Blue Book.

56
00:01:43,574 --> 00:01:46,602
Total ownership costs come from AAA's yearly

57
00:01:46,602 --> 00:01:48,323
Your Driving Costs study.

58
00:01:48,503 --> 00:01:50,988
These numbers change every quarter,

59
00:01:50,988 --> 00:01:53,402
so treat them as recent snapshots,

60
00:01:53,402 --> 00:01:54,609
not exact quotes.

61
00:01:54,789 --> 00:01:56,322
And this is education,

62
00:01:56,322 --> 00:01:57,785
not financial advice.

63
00:01:58,135 --> 00:02:00,662
Car loans are almost as old as

64
00:02:00,662 --> 00:02:01,083
cars.

65
00:02:01,263 --> 00:02:02,885
In the nineteen twenties,

66
00:02:02,885 --> 00:02:05,933
as cars became affordable to ordinary families,

67
00:02:05,933 --> 00:02:08,722
companies began offering installment plans,

68
00:02:08,722 --> 00:02:10,668
so people could pay over time.

69
00:02:10,848 --> 00:02:13,526
General Motors created its own finance arm

70
00:02:13,526 --> 00:02:14,865
in nineteen nineteen.

71
00:02:15,045 --> 00:02:16,351
Within a few years,

72
00:02:16,351 --> 00:02:18,276
a large share of new cars in

73
00:02:18,276 --> 00:02:20,339
America were bought on credit.

74
00:02:20,519 --> 00:02:22,880
The car payment was born almost as

75
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:24,478
soon as the car itself.

76
00:02:24,828 --> 00:02:27,672
For decades, loans were relatively short.

77
00:02:27,852 --> 00:02:29,924
Three year loans were common for a

78
00:02:29,924 --> 00:02:30,534
long time.

79
00:02:30,714 --> 00:02:33,101
As prices rose faster than incomes,

80
00:02:33,101 --> 00:02:36,647
lenders stretched terms to keep payments manageable.

81
00:02:36,827 --> 00:02:38,465
Four years, then five,

82
00:02:38,465 --> 00:02:40,847
then six, and now seven years or

83
00:02:40,847 --> 00:02:42,411
more for some buyers.

84
00:02:42,591 --> 00:02:45,302
Each step made expensive cars feel affordable,

85
00:02:45,302 --> 00:02:47,189
without making them any cheaper.

86
00:02:47,539 --> 00:02:48,749
At the same time,

87
00:02:48,749 --> 00:02:49,675
cars changed.

88
00:02:49,855 --> 00:02:51,078
They became safer,

89
00:02:51,078 --> 00:02:53,456
more comfortable and more reliable,

90
00:02:53,456 --> 00:02:54,476
which is great.

91
00:02:54,656 --> 00:02:57,019
But they also became more expensive,

92
00:02:57,019 --> 00:02:59,777
and the most popular vehicles shifted from

93
00:02:59,777 --> 00:03:02,469
smaller sedans to larger trucks and SUVs.

94
00:03:02,649 --> 00:03:05,788
Many automakers reduced or dropped their cheapest

95
00:03:05,788 --> 00:03:06,237
models.

96
00:03:06,417 --> 00:03:09,175
So today's buyer often faces fewer low

97
00:03:09,175 --> 00:03:12,152
cost choices, and a bigger default price.

98
00:03:12,502 --> 00:03:13,210
Angle one.

99
00:03:13,390 --> 00:03:14,191
The numbers.

100
00:03:14,371 --> 00:03:16,751
Let's see what a car actually costs.

101
00:03:17,101 --> 00:03:17,890
Number one.

102
00:03:18,070 --> 00:03:19,185
The payment itself.

103
00:03:19,535 --> 00:03:22,262
According to industry data from firms like

104
00:03:22,262 --> 00:03:23,625
Edmunds and Experian,

105
00:03:23,625 --> 00:03:25,963
the average monthly payment on a new

106
00:03:25,963 --> 00:03:28,430
car loan has been around seven hundred

107
00:03:28,430 --> 00:03:30,378
fifty dollars in recent years.

108
00:03:30,558 --> 00:03:33,077
Used car payments have averaged around five

109
00:03:33,077 --> 00:03:34,482
hundred dollars or more.

110
00:03:34,662 --> 00:03:36,660
And a growing share of new car

111
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:38,724
buyers, close to one in five in

112
00:03:38,724 --> 00:03:40,055
some recent reports,

113
00:03:40,055 --> 00:03:42,585
have taken on payments of one thousand

114
00:03:42,585 --> 00:03:44,183
dollars a month or more.

115
00:03:44,533 --> 00:03:46,030
Think about what that means.

116
00:03:46,210 --> 00:03:48,383
Seven hundred fifty dollars a month is

117
00:03:48,383 --> 00:03:50,042
nine thousand dollars a year.

118
00:03:50,222 --> 00:03:53,080
For a household earning sixty thousand dollars

119
00:03:53,080 --> 00:03:55,193
before taxes, that can be close to

120
00:03:55,193 --> 00:03:56,870
one fifth of take home pay,

121
00:03:56,870 --> 00:03:57,989
just for the loan.

122
00:03:58,169 --> 00:03:59,952
Before gas, insurance,

123
00:03:59,952 --> 00:04:02,627
repairs, registration or parking.

124
00:04:02,977 --> 00:04:04,892
And here's the important part.

125
00:04:05,072 --> 00:04:07,264
A car payment isn't like a mortgage,

126
00:04:07,264 --> 00:04:10,125
where you're often building equity in something

127
00:04:10,125 --> 00:04:11,585
that may hold its value.

128
00:04:11,765 --> 00:04:14,981
A car is almost always losing value.

129
00:04:15,161 --> 00:04:17,089
You're making a big payment on something

130
00:04:17,089 --> 00:04:19,016
that's shrinking every month you own it.

131
00:04:19,366 --> 00:04:20,225
Number two.

132
00:04:20,405 --> 00:04:21,101
The price.

133
00:04:21,452 --> 00:04:23,661
Kelley Blue Book data shows the average

134
00:04:23,661 --> 00:04:25,474
price paid for a new vehicle has

135
00:04:25,474 --> 00:04:28,081
been close to fifty thousand dollars recently.

136
00:04:28,261 --> 00:04:30,502
Trucks and SUVs have become the most

137
00:04:30,502 --> 00:04:32,930
popular vehicles, and they tend to cost

138
00:04:32,930 --> 00:04:33,242
more.

139
00:04:33,422 --> 00:04:36,280
Features that used to be luxury options,

140
00:04:36,280 --> 00:04:39,496
like big screens and advanced safety systems,

141
00:04:39,496 --> 00:04:41,711
are now standard in many trims.

142
00:04:42,061 --> 00:04:43,674
Compare that with incomes.

143
00:04:43,854 --> 00:04:46,262
The median household income in the United

144
00:04:46,262 --> 00:04:48,553
States, according to the Census Bureau,

145
00:04:48,553 --> 00:04:50,902
has been around eighty thousand dollars.

146
00:04:51,082 --> 00:04:53,314
So the typical new car now costs

147
00:04:53,314 --> 00:04:56,036
well over half of a typical household's

148
00:04:56,036 --> 00:04:57,990
yearly income, before taxes.

149
00:04:58,170 --> 00:05:00,748
Many planners suggest your total car costs

150
00:05:00,748 --> 00:05:02,775
should be a much smaller share of

151
00:05:02,775 --> 00:05:04,125
your budget than that.

152
00:05:04,476 --> 00:05:05,346
Number three.

153
00:05:05,526 --> 00:05:06,327
The loan term.

154
00:05:06,678 --> 00:05:08,992
To make big prices fit into monthly

155
00:05:08,992 --> 00:05:11,240
budgets, loans have gotten longer.

156
00:05:11,420 --> 00:05:12,508
Six year loans,

157
00:05:12,508 --> 00:05:13,885
seventy two months,

158
00:05:13,885 --> 00:05:14,973
are now common.

159
00:05:15,153 --> 00:05:16,380
Seven year loans,

160
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:17,752
eighty four months,

161
00:05:17,752 --> 00:05:19,774
have become more common too.

162
00:05:19,954 --> 00:05:22,500
A longer loan lowers the monthly payment,

163
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:25,294
which makes an expensive car feel affordable.

164
00:05:25,645 --> 00:05:28,025
But longer loans have two big costs.

165
00:05:28,205 --> 00:05:29,881
First, more total interest,

166
00:05:29,881 --> 00:05:32,117
because you're borrowing for longer.

167
00:05:32,297 --> 00:05:34,378
Second, you can owe more than the

168
00:05:34,378 --> 00:05:36,206
car is worth for much longer,

169
00:05:36,206 --> 00:05:38,665
because the car loses value faster than

170
00:05:38,665 --> 00:05:40,053
you pay down the loan.

171
00:05:40,233 --> 00:05:42,523
That gap is called negative equity,

172
00:05:42,523 --> 00:05:44,093
and we'll get to it in a

173
00:05:44,093 --> 00:05:44,552
moment.

174
00:05:44,902 --> 00:05:47,601
Same forty thousand dollar loan at seven

175
00:05:47,601 --> 00:05:48,141
percent.

176
00:05:48,321 --> 00:05:49,305
Over five years,

177
00:05:49,305 --> 00:05:51,826
the payment is about seven hundred ninety

178
00:05:51,826 --> 00:05:54,410
dollars and the total interest about seven

179
00:05:54,410 --> 00:05:55,763
thousand five hundred.

180
00:05:55,943 --> 00:05:57,076
Over seven years,

181
00:05:57,076 --> 00:05:59,608
the payment drops to about six hundred

182
00:05:59,608 --> 00:06:02,207
five dollars, but the interest rises to

183
00:06:02,207 --> 00:06:04,406
about ten thousand seven hundred.

184
00:06:04,757 --> 00:06:05,453
Number four.

185
00:06:05,633 --> 00:06:06,852
The interest rate.

186
00:06:07,203 --> 00:06:09,350
When interest rates were near zero,

187
00:06:09,350 --> 00:06:10,639
car loans were cheap.

188
00:06:10,819 --> 00:06:11,875
In recent years,

189
00:06:11,875 --> 00:06:13,129
rates have climbed.

190
00:06:13,309 --> 00:06:16,028
New car loans have often averaged around

191
00:06:16,028 --> 00:06:18,611
seven percent, and used car loans have

192
00:06:18,611 --> 00:06:20,786
often been in the double digits.

193
00:06:20,966 --> 00:06:23,510
Borrowers with lower credit scores can pay

194
00:06:23,510 --> 00:06:26,235
far more, sometimes fifteen to twenty percent

195
00:06:26,235 --> 00:06:26,841
or higher.

196
00:06:27,191 --> 00:06:28,113
At those rates,

197
00:06:28,113 --> 00:06:30,449
the same car can cost wildly different

198
00:06:30,449 --> 00:06:30,941
amounts.

199
00:06:31,121 --> 00:06:33,579
A thirty thousand dollar used car loan

200
00:06:33,579 --> 00:06:35,972
at seven percent for five years costs

201
00:06:35,972 --> 00:06:38,689
about five thousand six hundred dollars in

202
00:06:38,689 --> 00:06:39,271
interest.

203
00:06:39,451 --> 00:06:40,642
At fifteen percent,

204
00:06:40,642 --> 00:06:43,212
it's about twelve thousand eight hundred.

205
00:06:43,392 --> 00:06:44,344
Same car.

206
00:06:44,524 --> 00:06:46,428
More than double the interest.

207
00:06:46,608 --> 00:06:49,142
Your credit score is literally priced into

208
00:06:49,142 --> 00:06:49,685
your car.

209
00:06:50,035 --> 00:06:50,952
Number five.

210
00:06:51,132 --> 00:06:52,269
Depreciation.

211
00:06:52,449 --> 00:06:54,586
The cost nobody sends you a bill

212
00:06:54,586 --> 00:06:54,853
for.

213
00:06:55,202 --> 00:06:58,008
Depreciation is the value a car loses

214
00:06:58,008 --> 00:06:58,767
over time.

215
00:06:58,947 --> 00:07:01,560
It's often the single biggest cost of

216
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:02,761
owning a new car,

217
00:07:02,761 --> 00:07:04,810
bigger than gas or insurance.

218
00:07:04,990 --> 00:07:07,204
A common estimate is that many new

219
00:07:07,204 --> 00:07:09,809
cars lose around twenty percent of their

220
00:07:09,809 --> 00:07:11,372
value in the first year,

221
00:07:11,372 --> 00:07:13,521
and roughly half or more in about

222
00:07:13,521 --> 00:07:15,735
five years, though it varies a lot

223
00:07:15,735 --> 00:07:16,321
by model.

224
00:07:16,671 --> 00:07:18,601
On a fifty thousand dollar car,

225
00:07:18,601 --> 00:07:21,029
losing twenty percent in the first year

226
00:07:21,029 --> 00:07:22,523
is ten thousand dollars.

227
00:07:22,703 --> 00:07:24,966
That's almost two hundred dollars a week,

228
00:07:24,966 --> 00:07:27,009
gone, just for the privilege of being

229
00:07:27,009 --> 00:07:27,893
the first owner.

230
00:07:28,073 --> 00:07:29,535
You never see it on a bill.

231
00:07:29,715 --> 00:07:31,691
You only find out when you sell

232
00:07:31,691 --> 00:07:32,455
or trade in.

233
00:07:32,805 --> 00:07:34,768
This is why many wealthy people buy

234
00:07:34,768 --> 00:07:36,451
cars that are a few years old.

235
00:07:36,631 --> 00:07:38,562
The first owner has already paid for

236
00:07:38,562 --> 00:07:40,172
the steepest part of the drop.

237
00:07:40,352 --> 00:07:41,841
You still get a reliable,

238
00:07:41,841 --> 00:07:43,806
modern car, and you skip the most

239
00:07:43,806 --> 00:07:45,414
expensive part of the ride.

240
00:07:45,763 --> 00:07:46,820
Number six.

241
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:48,753
The full cost of owning.

242
00:07:49,103 --> 00:07:52,233
AAA calculates the total yearly cost of

243
00:07:52,233 --> 00:07:53,597
owning a new car,

244
00:07:53,597 --> 00:07:56,326
including depreciation, insurance,

245
00:07:56,326 --> 00:07:59,135
fuel, maintenance, finance charges,

246
00:07:59,135 --> 00:08:00,980
registration and taxes.

247
00:08:01,160 --> 00:08:02,102
In recent years,

248
00:08:02,102 --> 00:08:04,337
its estimate has been more than eleven

249
00:08:04,337 --> 00:08:06,573
thousand dollars a year for an average

250
00:08:06,573 --> 00:08:09,044
new vehicle driven fifteen thousand miles.

251
00:08:09,224 --> 00:08:11,348
That's roughly nine hundred to one thousand

252
00:08:11,348 --> 00:08:12,138
dollars a month.

253
00:08:12,487 --> 00:08:14,889
So when someone says their car payment

254
00:08:14,889 --> 00:08:16,406
is five hundred dollars,

255
00:08:16,406 --> 00:08:18,618
the real cost is often double that.

256
00:08:18,798 --> 00:08:21,632
Car insurance alone has risen sharply in

257
00:08:21,632 --> 00:08:23,616
recent years in many states.

258
00:08:23,796 --> 00:08:27,127
Bigger, more expensive cars usually mean bigger

259
00:08:27,127 --> 00:08:29,254
insurance bills, bigger tires,

260
00:08:29,254 --> 00:08:31,168
and more expensive repairs.

261
00:08:31,518 --> 00:08:33,723
Insurance deserves its own warning.

262
00:08:33,903 --> 00:08:34,958
In recent years,

263
00:08:34,958 --> 00:08:37,925
car insurance premiums have risen much faster

264
00:08:37,925 --> 00:08:40,496
than general inflation in many parts of

265
00:08:40,496 --> 00:08:41,287
the country.

266
00:08:41,467 --> 00:08:43,827
Modern cars are full of sensors,

267
00:08:43,827 --> 00:08:45,449
cameras and computers,

268
00:08:45,449 --> 00:08:48,915
which makes even small accidents more expensive

269
00:08:48,915 --> 00:08:49,652
to repair.

270
00:08:49,832 --> 00:08:53,211
And repair costs feed directly into premiums.

271
00:08:53,391 --> 00:08:55,994
Some drivers have seen their insurance rise

272
00:08:55,994 --> 00:08:57,810
by hundreds of dollars a year,

273
00:08:57,810 --> 00:08:59,324
even with a clean record.

274
00:08:59,674 --> 00:09:01,612
Try this simple exercise.

275
00:09:01,792 --> 00:09:03,707
Add up everything your car cost you

276
00:09:03,707 --> 00:09:04,254
last year.

277
00:09:04,434 --> 00:09:06,742
Payments, insurance, fuel,

278
00:09:06,742 --> 00:09:09,848
maintenance, repairs, registration,

279
00:09:09,848 --> 00:09:11,446
parking and tolls.

280
00:09:11,626 --> 00:09:13,658
Then divide by the miles you drove.

281
00:09:13,838 --> 00:09:16,726
Many people discover they're paying sixty,

282
00:09:16,726 --> 00:09:19,202
eighty cents or more for every mile.

283
00:09:19,382 --> 00:09:21,311
Once you know your cost per mile,

284
00:09:21,311 --> 00:09:23,592
every trip and every car decision looks

285
00:09:23,592 --> 00:09:24,177
different.

286
00:09:24,527 --> 00:09:25,798
According to AAA,

287
00:09:25,798 --> 00:09:28,492
owning an average new car costs more

288
00:09:28,492 --> 00:09:31,185
than eleven thousand dollars a year,

289
00:09:31,185 --> 00:09:33,131
once you add depreciation,

290
00:09:33,131 --> 00:09:35,301
insurance, fuel, maintenance,

291
00:09:35,301 --> 00:09:36,647
interest and fees.

292
00:09:36,998 --> 00:09:37,973
Number seven.

293
00:09:38,153 --> 00:09:39,209
Negative equity.

294
00:09:39,560 --> 00:09:42,642
Negative equity, also called being upside down

295
00:09:42,642 --> 00:09:45,055
or underwater, means you owe more on

296
00:09:45,055 --> 00:09:47,199
your loan than the car is worth.

297
00:09:47,379 --> 00:09:49,841
With long loans and fast depreciation,

298
00:09:49,841 --> 00:09:51,396
it's become very common.

299
00:09:51,576 --> 00:09:53,995
Edmunds has reported that around a quarter

300
00:09:53,995 --> 00:09:55,895
or more of new car buyers trading

301
00:09:55,895 --> 00:09:57,622
in a vehicle owed more than it

302
00:09:57,622 --> 00:10:00,214
was worth, often by several thousand dollars.

303
00:10:00,564 --> 00:10:02,556
What do people do with that negative

304
00:10:02,556 --> 00:10:02,944
equity?

305
00:10:03,124 --> 00:10:05,244
Very often, they roll it into the

306
00:10:05,244 --> 00:10:05,887
next loan.

307
00:10:06,067 --> 00:10:07,971
The dealer adds the old debt to

308
00:10:07,971 --> 00:10:09,016
the new car loan.

309
00:10:09,196 --> 00:10:11,208
Now you start your new car already

310
00:10:11,208 --> 00:10:13,635
deeply underwater, with a bigger loan and

311
00:10:13,635 --> 00:10:14,641
a bigger payment.

312
00:10:14,821 --> 00:10:16,462
And if you trade in again in

313
00:10:16,462 --> 00:10:17,166
a few years,

314
00:10:17,166 --> 00:10:18,397
the hole gets deeper.

315
00:10:18,747 --> 00:10:21,712
Negative equity is also dangerous if the

316
00:10:21,712 --> 00:10:23,565
car is totaled or stolen.

317
00:10:23,745 --> 00:10:26,139
Insurance pays what the car is worth,

318
00:10:26,139 --> 00:10:27,239
not what you owe.

319
00:10:27,419 --> 00:10:28,742
Without gap insurance,

320
00:10:28,742 --> 00:10:30,546
you could be left paying off a

321
00:10:30,546 --> 00:10:32,169
loan on a car you no longer

322
00:10:32,169 --> 00:10:32,470
have.

323
00:10:32,820 --> 00:10:33,655
Number eight.

324
00:10:33,835 --> 00:10:35,159
The opportunity cost.

325
00:10:35,339 --> 00:10:38,009
Here's the hundred thousand dollar car payment

326
00:10:38,009 --> 00:10:38,648
I promised.

327
00:10:38,998 --> 00:10:41,081
Opportunity cost is what you give up

328
00:10:41,081 --> 00:10:43,107
by choosing one thing over another.

329
00:10:43,287 --> 00:10:46,003
Imagine the difference between a seven hundred

330
00:10:46,003 --> 00:10:48,247
fifty dollar payment and a more modest

331
00:10:48,247 --> 00:10:50,549
three hundred fifty dollar payment on a

332
00:10:50,549 --> 00:10:51,612
reliable used car.

333
00:10:51,792 --> 00:10:53,440
That's four hundred dollars a month.

334
00:10:53,791 --> 00:10:56,329
Now imagine you invest that four hundred

335
00:10:56,329 --> 00:10:57,598
dollars every month,

336
00:10:57,598 --> 00:11:00,200
at a seven percent average yearly return,

337
00:11:00,200 --> 00:11:01,279
for thirty years.

338
00:11:01,459 --> 00:11:03,064
The math says it could grow to

339
00:11:03,064 --> 00:11:05,418
nearly four hundred ninety thousand dollars.

340
00:11:05,598 --> 00:11:07,065
Even over fifteen years,

341
00:11:07,065 --> 00:11:09,204
it could be over one hundred twenty

342
00:11:09,204 --> 00:11:09,754
thousand.

343
00:11:09,934 --> 00:11:11,846
That's the real price of the nicer

344
00:11:11,846 --> 00:11:12,071
car.

345
00:11:12,251 --> 00:11:13,156
Not the sticker.

346
00:11:13,336 --> 00:11:14,938
The future it replaces.

347
00:11:15,289 --> 00:11:17,306
Four hundred dollars a month,

348
00:11:17,306 --> 00:11:19,602
the gap between a big car payment

349
00:11:19,602 --> 00:11:20,785
and a modest one,

350
00:11:20,785 --> 00:11:23,289
invested at seven percent a year for

351
00:11:23,289 --> 00:11:24,193
thirty years.

352
00:11:24,373 --> 00:11:26,296
You'd put in one hundred forty four

353
00:11:26,296 --> 00:11:27,229
thousand dollars.

354
00:11:27,580 --> 00:11:29,160
That's an illustration,

355
00:11:29,160 --> 00:11:30,122
not a promise.

356
00:11:30,302 --> 00:11:31,893
Markets go up and down.

357
00:11:32,073 --> 00:11:34,131
But it shows why many self made

358
00:11:34,131 --> 00:11:36,322
millionaires drive ordinary cars.

359
00:11:36,502 --> 00:11:38,097
It's not that they can't afford a

360
00:11:38,097 --> 00:11:38,580
nicer one.

361
00:11:38,760 --> 00:11:40,703
It's that they know what the nicer

362
00:11:40,703 --> 00:11:41,674
one really costs.

363
00:11:42,025 --> 00:11:44,579
And here's the part that worries economists.

364
00:11:44,759 --> 00:11:47,368
When people stretch for big car payments,

365
00:11:47,368 --> 00:11:49,659
there's less room for anything to go

366
00:11:49,659 --> 00:11:50,041
wrong.

367
00:11:50,221 --> 00:11:53,977
Credit agencies have reported rising delinquencies on

368
00:11:53,977 --> 00:11:57,449
auto loans, especially among borrowers with lower

369
00:11:57,449 --> 00:11:58,441
credit scores.

370
00:11:58,621 --> 00:12:01,362
Missing car payments can lead to repossession,

371
00:12:01,362 --> 00:12:03,209
which damages credit for years,

372
00:12:03,209 --> 00:12:05,653
and often leaves people still owing money

373
00:12:05,653 --> 00:12:07,619
after the car is sold at auction.

374
00:12:07,969 --> 00:12:09,606
So the numbers are clear.

375
00:12:09,786 --> 00:12:11,354
But if they're so clear,

376
00:12:11,354 --> 00:12:13,315
why do so many people fall in?

377
00:12:13,495 --> 00:12:14,482
That's angle two.

378
00:12:14,832 --> 00:12:15,876
Angle two.

379
00:12:16,056 --> 00:12:17,229
The psychology.

380
00:12:17,409 --> 00:12:19,699
Six ways the car buying process,

381
00:12:19,699 --> 00:12:21,059
and our own brains,

382
00:12:21,059 --> 00:12:22,634
push us into the trap.

383
00:12:22,984 --> 00:12:24,168
Trick number nine.

384
00:12:24,348 --> 00:12:25,869
The monthly payment question.

385
00:12:26,219 --> 00:12:28,294
Walk into many dealerships and one of

386
00:12:28,294 --> 00:12:29,584
the first questions is,

387
00:12:29,584 --> 00:12:31,884
what monthly payment are you looking for?

388
00:12:32,064 --> 00:12:33,144
It sounds helpful.

389
00:12:33,324 --> 00:12:34,457
But once you answer,

390
00:12:34,457 --> 00:12:36,778
the conversation can shift from the price

391
00:12:36,778 --> 00:12:38,194
of the car to the size of

392
00:12:38,194 --> 00:12:38,874
the payment.

393
00:12:39,054 --> 00:12:41,320
And almost any car can fit almost

394
00:12:41,320 --> 00:12:43,519
any payment, if the loan is long

395
00:12:43,519 --> 00:12:43,999
enough.

396
00:12:44,350 --> 00:12:46,439
Say you answer five hundred dollars.

397
00:12:46,619 --> 00:12:48,831
The dealer can stretch the loan,

398
00:12:48,831 --> 00:12:50,629
adjust the trade in value,

399
00:12:50,629 --> 00:12:51,942
add a few products,

400
00:12:51,942 --> 00:12:54,085
and still land at five hundred.

401
00:12:54,265 --> 00:12:55,727
You feel like you got what you

402
00:12:55,727 --> 00:12:56,215
asked for.

403
00:12:56,395 --> 00:12:57,538
But the total cost,

404
00:12:57,538 --> 00:12:59,463
the thing that actually matters,

405
00:12:59,463 --> 00:13:01,689
might be thousands of dollars higher.

406
00:13:02,040 --> 00:13:04,649
Try this: when asked about payments,

407
00:13:04,649 --> 00:13:06,896
say, I'm focused on the out the

408
00:13:06,896 --> 00:13:07,694
door price.

409
00:13:07,874 --> 00:13:10,416
That's the total price including taxes and

410
00:13:10,416 --> 00:13:10,718
fees.

411
00:13:10,898 --> 00:13:12,454
Negotiate that first.

412
00:13:12,634 --> 00:13:15,118
Financing is a separate conversation.

413
00:13:15,469 --> 00:13:16,606
Trick number ten.

414
00:13:16,786 --> 00:13:18,621
Juggling four numbers at once.

415
00:13:18,971 --> 00:13:21,325
Car deals involve four main numbers:

416
00:13:21,325 --> 00:13:22,698
the price of the car,

417
00:13:22,698 --> 00:13:24,463
the value of your trade in,

418
00:13:24,463 --> 00:13:25,575
the down payment,

419
00:13:25,575 --> 00:13:27,144
and the monthly payment.

420
00:13:27,324 --> 00:13:30,690
Some salespeople have traditionally used a worksheet

421
00:13:30,690 --> 00:13:33,473
sometimes called the four square to discuss

422
00:13:33,473 --> 00:13:34,638
all four together.

423
00:13:34,818 --> 00:13:36,440
When everything moves at once,

424
00:13:36,440 --> 00:13:38,278
it's very hard to see where you're

425
00:13:38,278 --> 00:13:40,008
gaining and where you're losing.

426
00:13:40,358 --> 00:13:42,390
A great trade in value can be

427
00:13:42,390 --> 00:13:44,142
offset by a higher price.

428
00:13:44,322 --> 00:13:46,434
A lower price can be offset by

429
00:13:46,434 --> 00:13:47,701
a worse loan rate.

430
00:13:47,881 --> 00:13:50,126
Confusion helps the side that does this

431
00:13:50,126 --> 00:13:50,702
every day.

432
00:13:50,882 --> 00:13:53,513
Try this: negotiate one number at a

433
00:13:53,513 --> 00:13:53,889
time.

434
00:13:54,069 --> 00:13:56,171
First the out the door price.

435
00:13:56,351 --> 00:13:58,498
Then, separately, your trade in.

436
00:13:58,678 --> 00:13:59,758
Then financing.

437
00:13:59,938 --> 00:14:01,088
Never all at once.

438
00:14:01,438 --> 00:14:02,889
Trick number eleven.

439
00:14:03,069 --> 00:14:03,835
The add ons.

440
00:14:04,185 --> 00:14:05,640
After you agree on a car,

441
00:14:05,640 --> 00:14:08,027
you're often taken to the finance office.

442
00:14:08,207 --> 00:14:09,577
That's where add ons appear.

443
00:14:09,757 --> 00:14:13,419
Extended warranties, paint and fabric protection,

444
00:14:13,419 --> 00:14:14,988
tire and wheel plans,

445
00:14:14,988 --> 00:14:16,781
gap insurance, and more.

446
00:14:16,961 --> 00:14:18,192
Some can be useful.

447
00:14:18,372 --> 00:14:20,861
But they're often priced with big markups,

448
00:14:20,861 --> 00:14:23,291
and they're usually rolled into the loan,

449
00:14:23,291 --> 00:14:25,187
so you pay interest on them too.

450
00:14:25,538 --> 00:14:27,376
Gap insurance, for example,

451
00:14:27,376 --> 00:14:29,146
can be smart if you have a

452
00:14:29,146 --> 00:14:31,528
small down payment and a long loan.

453
00:14:31,708 --> 00:14:33,676
But it's often cheaper through your own

454
00:14:33,676 --> 00:14:35,493
auto insurer than at the dealership.

455
00:14:35,673 --> 00:14:38,955
Extended warranties can sometimes be bought later,

456
00:14:38,955 --> 00:14:40,398
or from other sources.

457
00:14:40,578 --> 00:14:42,313
Try this: before you go,

458
00:14:42,313 --> 00:14:44,480
decide which add ons you want,

459
00:14:44,480 --> 00:14:46,720
if any, and get outside quotes.

460
00:14:46,900 --> 00:14:47,761
In the office,

461
00:14:47,761 --> 00:14:48,683
you can say no,

462
00:14:48,683 --> 00:14:50,035
and you can walk away.

463
00:14:50,385 --> 00:14:51,522
Trick number twelve.

464
00:14:51,702 --> 00:14:52,457
Identity.

465
00:14:52,807 --> 00:14:54,827
Cars aren't just transportation.

466
00:14:55,007 --> 00:14:55,924
They're identity.

467
00:14:56,104 --> 00:14:58,584
Car ads rarely talk about cost per

468
00:14:58,584 --> 00:14:58,948
mile.

469
00:14:59,128 --> 00:15:00,477
They show freedom,

470
00:15:00,477 --> 00:15:02,426
power, adventure, success.

471
00:15:02,606 --> 00:15:03,719
And for many people,

472
00:15:03,719 --> 00:15:05,390
the car in the driveway is the

473
00:15:05,390 --> 00:15:07,617
most visible signal of how they're doing

474
00:15:07,617 --> 00:15:08,062
in life.

475
00:15:08,242 --> 00:15:10,317
That makes cars a perfect target for

476
00:15:10,317 --> 00:15:11,470
comparison spending.

477
00:15:11,820 --> 00:15:14,071
The tricky part is that this signal

478
00:15:14,071 --> 00:15:15,036
is often false.

479
00:15:15,216 --> 00:15:17,839
A new luxury SUV might mean someone

480
00:15:17,839 --> 00:15:18,664
is wealthy.

481
00:15:18,844 --> 00:15:20,673
Or it might mean they have an

482
00:15:20,673 --> 00:15:23,070
eighty four month loan and no savings.

483
00:15:23,250 --> 00:15:26,477
Thomas Stanley's research on millionaires found many

484
00:15:26,477 --> 00:15:27,780
of them drove modest,

485
00:15:27,780 --> 00:15:28,463
older cars.

486
00:15:28,643 --> 00:15:31,159
The flashiest car on the street is

487
00:15:31,159 --> 00:15:33,379
not a reliable sign of wealth.

488
00:15:33,559 --> 00:15:35,587
Try this: decide what you want your

489
00:15:35,587 --> 00:15:37,441
money to say about you in twenty

490
00:15:37,441 --> 00:15:39,527
years, not in the parking lot today.

491
00:15:39,877 --> 00:15:41,328
Trick number thirteen.

492
00:15:41,508 --> 00:15:42,866
The new car feeling.

493
00:15:43,217 --> 00:15:45,132
A new car feels amazing.

494
00:15:45,312 --> 00:15:47,339
The smell, the technology,

495
00:15:47,339 --> 00:15:48,586
the smooth ride.

496
00:15:48,766 --> 00:15:51,760
But psychologists have long studied how quickly

497
00:15:51,760 --> 00:15:53,480
people adapt to new things.

498
00:15:53,660 --> 00:15:55,692
It's called hedonic adaptation.

499
00:15:55,872 --> 00:15:58,192
Within months, the new car becomes just

500
00:15:58,192 --> 00:15:58,728
your car.

501
00:15:58,908 --> 00:16:00,405
The excitement fades.

502
00:16:00,585 --> 00:16:01,572
The payment doesn't.

503
00:16:01,923 --> 00:16:04,209
That's how many people end up trading

504
00:16:04,209 --> 00:16:06,002
in after three or four years,

505
00:16:06,002 --> 00:16:07,609
chasing the feeling again,

506
00:16:07,609 --> 00:16:10,019
often while still underwater on the old

507
00:16:10,019 --> 00:16:10,328
loan.

508
00:16:10,508 --> 00:16:11,809
The trap repeats.

509
00:16:11,989 --> 00:16:13,632
Try this: before buying,

510
00:16:13,632 --> 00:16:15,959
imagine the car two years from now

511
00:16:15,959 --> 00:16:18,629
with a few scratches and coffee stains.

512
00:16:18,809 --> 00:16:20,342
Is it still worth the payment?

513
00:16:20,522 --> 00:16:22,147
If the answer is yes,

514
00:16:22,147 --> 00:16:22,612
great.

515
00:16:22,792 --> 00:16:25,339
If it's only exciting when it's new,

516
00:16:25,339 --> 00:16:25,764
pause.

517
00:16:26,114 --> 00:16:27,669
Trick number fourteen.

518
00:16:27,849 --> 00:16:30,020
Thinking everyone has a car payment.

519
00:16:30,371 --> 00:16:32,741
Many people believe a car payment is

520
00:16:32,741 --> 00:16:34,782
a permanent part of adult life,

521
00:16:34,782 --> 00:16:35,967
like a phone bill.

522
00:16:36,147 --> 00:16:38,120
As soon as one loan ends,

523
00:16:38,120 --> 00:16:39,699
the next one starts.

524
00:16:39,879 --> 00:16:41,573
But that belief is one of the

525
00:16:41,573 --> 00:16:43,618
most expensive in personal finance.

526
00:16:43,798 --> 00:16:45,599
The years after a loan ends,

527
00:16:45,599 --> 00:16:47,787
when you still have a reliable car

528
00:16:47,787 --> 00:16:48,752
and no payment,

529
00:16:48,752 --> 00:16:50,682
are where real savings happen.

530
00:16:51,033 --> 00:16:52,609
If you keep a car for ten

531
00:16:52,609 --> 00:16:53,995
years instead of five,

532
00:16:53,995 --> 00:16:55,130
and it's reliable,

533
00:16:55,130 --> 00:16:57,274
you might enjoy five years with no

534
00:16:57,274 --> 00:16:58,219
payment at all.

535
00:16:58,399 --> 00:17:00,645
Even with higher repair costs in later

536
00:17:00,645 --> 00:17:03,009
years, those payment free years can free

537
00:17:03,009 --> 00:17:04,901
up tens of thousands of dollars.

538
00:17:05,081 --> 00:17:07,053
Try this: when your loan ends,

539
00:17:07,053 --> 00:17:08,894
keep paying the same amount,

540
00:17:08,894 --> 00:17:09,946
but to yourself,

541
00:17:09,946 --> 00:17:11,327
in a savings account.

542
00:17:11,507 --> 00:17:13,424
That becomes the cash for your next

543
00:17:13,424 --> 00:17:13,643
car.

544
00:17:13,993 --> 00:17:15,049
Quick pause.

545
00:17:15,229 --> 00:17:16,983
If this is changing how you look

546
00:17:16,983 --> 00:17:18,573
at that car in your driveway,

547
00:17:18,573 --> 00:17:19,943
subscribe to the channel.

548
00:17:20,123 --> 00:17:21,904
It's free, and it helps me keep

549
00:17:21,904 --> 00:17:23,513
making these videos for you.

550
00:17:23,693 --> 00:17:24,993
Now, the playbook.

551
00:17:25,344 --> 00:17:26,214
Angle three.

552
00:17:26,394 --> 00:17:27,195
The playbook.

553
00:17:27,375 --> 00:17:28,490
Ten moves to buy,

554
00:17:28,490 --> 00:17:30,916
finance and own a car without falling

555
00:17:30,916 --> 00:17:31,834
into the trap.

556
00:17:32,184 --> 00:17:35,010
Many planners use a simple guideline called

557
00:17:35,010 --> 00:17:36,653
the twenty four ten rule.

558
00:17:36,833 --> 00:17:38,888
Put at least twenty percent down.

559
00:17:39,068 --> 00:17:41,251
Finance for no more than four years.

560
00:17:41,431 --> 00:17:43,756
And keep total car costs under ten

561
00:17:43,756 --> 00:17:45,738
percent of your gross income.

562
00:17:46,089 --> 00:17:46,762
Move one.

563
00:17:46,942 --> 00:17:48,959
Use the twenty four ten rule as

564
00:17:48,959 --> 00:17:49,740
a guardrail.

565
00:17:49,920 --> 00:17:50,842
It's not a law,

566
00:17:50,842 --> 00:17:52,747
and in some places it's hard to

567
00:17:52,747 --> 00:17:55,144
follow, especially with today's prices.

568
00:17:55,324 --> 00:17:56,730
But the further you are from it,

569
00:17:56,730 --> 00:17:57,960
the more risk you're taking.

570
00:17:58,140 --> 00:17:59,881
If a car only fits with zero

571
00:17:59,881 --> 00:18:01,186
down and seven years,

572
00:18:01,186 --> 00:18:03,425
that's a sign it's too expensive for

573
00:18:03,425 --> 00:18:04,792
your budget right now.

574
00:18:05,143 --> 00:18:05,979
Move two.

575
00:18:06,159 --> 00:18:08,086
Know the full cost before you shop.

576
00:18:08,266 --> 00:18:09,479
Add the payment,

577
00:18:09,479 --> 00:18:12,968
insurance, fuel, maintenance and registration.

578
00:18:13,148 --> 00:18:15,159
Call your insurer and get a quote

579
00:18:15,159 --> 00:18:17,293
for the exact model before you buy.

580
00:18:17,473 --> 00:18:19,695
Some models cost much more to insure

581
00:18:19,695 --> 00:18:22,165
than others, and that surprise can wreck

582
00:18:22,165 --> 00:18:22,720
a budget.

583
00:18:23,071 --> 00:18:23,941
Move three.

584
00:18:24,121 --> 00:18:26,862
Consider buying a reliable used car,

585
00:18:26,862 --> 00:18:28,080
a few years old.

586
00:18:28,260 --> 00:18:30,376
You skip the steepest part of the

587
00:18:30,376 --> 00:18:33,069
depreciation, and many modern cars can run

588
00:18:33,069 --> 00:18:35,762
well past one hundred fifty thousand miles

589
00:18:35,762 --> 00:18:36,724
with good care.

590
00:18:36,904 --> 00:18:39,683
Have an independent mechanic inspect it before

591
00:18:39,683 --> 00:18:40,166
you buy.

592
00:18:40,346 --> 00:18:42,881
That inspection often costs less than one

593
00:18:42,881 --> 00:18:45,292
car payment and can save you thousands.

594
00:18:45,643 --> 00:18:46,420
Move four.

595
00:18:46,600 --> 00:18:48,731
Get pre approved for a loan before

596
00:18:48,731 --> 00:18:50,549
you walk into the dealership,

597
00:18:50,549 --> 00:18:52,742
ideally from a credit union or your

598
00:18:52,742 --> 00:18:53,056
bank.

599
00:18:53,236 --> 00:18:54,524
Now you know your rate.

600
00:18:54,704 --> 00:18:56,327
If the dealer can beat it,

601
00:18:56,327 --> 00:18:56,701
great.

602
00:18:56,881 --> 00:18:58,901
If not, you have a backup.

603
00:18:59,081 --> 00:19:00,789
This one move takes away a lot

604
00:19:00,789 --> 00:19:03,122
of the dealer's power over the financing.

605
00:19:03,472 --> 00:19:04,342
Move five.

606
00:19:04,522 --> 00:19:06,461
Negotiate the out the door price,

607
00:19:06,461 --> 00:19:08,400
in writing, by email if possible.

608
00:19:08,580 --> 00:19:10,459
Contact several dealers,

609
00:19:10,459 --> 00:19:13,043
ask for their best total price on

610
00:19:13,043 --> 00:19:14,140
the exact car,

611
00:19:14,140 --> 00:19:15,627
including all fees.

612
00:19:15,807 --> 00:19:16,736
Let them compete.

613
00:19:16,916 --> 00:19:18,167
This takes some time,

614
00:19:18,167 --> 00:19:19,953
but it can save more money per

615
00:19:19,953 --> 00:19:22,335
hour than almost anything else you'll do

616
00:19:22,335 --> 00:19:22,930
that year.

617
00:19:23,280 --> 00:19:24,533
Move six.

618
00:19:24,713 --> 00:19:26,606
Handle your trade in separately.

619
00:19:26,786 --> 00:19:29,287
Get offers from online buyers and other

620
00:19:29,287 --> 00:19:31,595
dealers first, so you know what it's

621
00:19:31,595 --> 00:19:32,428
really worth.

622
00:19:32,608 --> 00:19:35,313
Sometimes selling privately gets you more.

623
00:19:35,493 --> 00:19:36,260
Move seven.

624
00:19:36,440 --> 00:19:38,251
Say no in the finance office to

625
00:19:38,251 --> 00:19:40,120
anything you didn't plan to buy.

626
00:19:40,300 --> 00:19:41,819
You can always ask for time to

627
00:19:41,819 --> 00:19:42,123
think.

628
00:19:42,473 --> 00:19:43,343
Move eight.

629
00:19:43,523 --> 00:19:45,346
Keep your car for a long time.

630
00:19:45,526 --> 00:19:47,258
Maintain it on schedule,

631
00:19:47,258 --> 00:19:48,557
follow the manual,

632
00:19:48,557 --> 00:19:51,516
fix small problems before they become big

633
00:19:51,516 --> 00:19:51,877
ones.

634
00:19:52,057 --> 00:19:54,325
The longer you drive a reliable car

635
00:19:54,325 --> 00:19:55,621
after it's paid off,

636
00:19:55,621 --> 00:19:57,955
the more money stays in your pocket.

637
00:19:58,305 --> 00:19:59,024
Move nine.

638
00:19:59,204 --> 00:20:00,888
Pay yourself a car payment.

639
00:20:01,068 --> 00:20:02,383
When your loan ends,

640
00:20:02,383 --> 00:20:04,158
or right now if you have no

641
00:20:04,158 --> 00:20:06,459
loan, send a set amount every month

642
00:20:06,459 --> 00:20:07,511
into a car fund.

643
00:20:07,691 --> 00:20:09,696
When it's time for the next car,

644
00:20:09,696 --> 00:20:10,760
you can pay cash,

645
00:20:10,760 --> 00:20:12,452
or make a big down payment.

646
00:20:12,632 --> 00:20:14,471
That's how you leave the payment cycle

647
00:20:14,471 --> 00:20:14,907
for good.

648
00:20:15,257 --> 00:20:17,957
Paying yourself three hundred fifty dollars a

649
00:20:17,957 --> 00:20:19,217
month for five years,

650
00:20:19,217 --> 00:20:21,496
at a modest four percent savings rate,

651
00:20:21,496 --> 00:20:24,196
builds roughly twenty three thousand dollars.

652
00:20:24,376 --> 00:20:26,327
That's a solid used car,

653
00:20:26,327 --> 00:20:26,977
in cash.

654
00:20:27,327 --> 00:20:28,464
And move ten.

655
00:20:28,644 --> 00:20:30,611
If you're already underwater,

656
00:20:30,611 --> 00:20:31,628
stop the cycle.

657
00:20:31,808 --> 00:20:34,346
Don't roll the negative equity into another

658
00:20:34,346 --> 00:20:34,641
loan.

659
00:20:34,821 --> 00:20:36,747
Keep the car and pay extra toward

660
00:20:36,747 --> 00:20:39,024
the principal until you're above water.

661
00:20:39,204 --> 00:20:40,789
Check if you can refinance at a

662
00:20:40,789 --> 00:20:41,352
lower rate.

663
00:20:41,532 --> 00:20:44,344
Or, if the payment is truly unaffordable,

664
00:20:44,344 --> 00:20:48,186
consider selling privately and buying something cheaper,

665
00:20:48,186 --> 00:20:50,518
covering the gap with savings or a

666
00:20:50,518 --> 00:20:51,272
small loan.

667
00:20:51,452 --> 00:20:52,474
It hurts once.

668
00:20:52,654 --> 00:20:54,860
Rolling it forward hurts for years.

669
00:20:55,210 --> 00:20:56,243
Ten moves.

670
00:20:56,423 --> 00:20:58,484
A guardrail, the full cost,

671
00:20:58,484 --> 00:21:00,621
slightly used, pre approval,

672
00:21:00,621 --> 00:21:02,071
out the door price,

673
00:21:02,071 --> 00:21:03,597
trade in separately,

674
00:21:03,597 --> 00:21:04,895
say no to extras,

675
00:21:04,895 --> 00:21:05,887
keep it long,

676
00:21:05,887 --> 00:21:08,711
pay yourself, and never roll the hole

677
00:21:08,711 --> 00:21:09,322
forward.

678
00:21:09,672 --> 00:21:11,580
Let me share a pattern I see

679
00:21:11,580 --> 00:21:14,716
again and again in how financially comfortable

680
00:21:14,716 --> 00:21:16,011
people handle cars.

681
00:21:16,191 --> 00:21:17,549
It's not glamorous.

682
00:21:17,729 --> 00:21:18,530
That's the point.

683
00:21:18,880 --> 00:21:21,340
First, they decide the budget before they

684
00:21:21,340 --> 00:21:22,781
fall in love with a car.

685
00:21:22,961 --> 00:21:24,674
The number comes from their plan,

686
00:21:24,674 --> 00:21:25,817
not from the showroom.

687
00:21:25,997 --> 00:21:28,173
They know what share of their income

688
00:21:28,173 --> 00:21:29,564
goes to transportation,

689
00:21:29,564 --> 00:21:31,559
and they shop inside that number,

690
00:21:31,559 --> 00:21:34,158
even if they could technically afford more.

691
00:21:34,509 --> 00:21:36,923
Second, they think in total cost,

692
00:21:36,923 --> 00:21:38,386
not monthly payment.

693
00:21:38,566 --> 00:21:40,746
They ask what the car will cost

694
00:21:40,746 --> 00:21:42,856
them over the next five or ten

695
00:21:42,856 --> 00:21:45,950
years, including depreciation and insurance.

696
00:21:46,130 --> 00:21:48,447
A car that's cheaper per month but

697
00:21:48,447 --> 00:21:50,900
loses value faster can easily be the

698
00:21:50,900 --> 00:21:52,400
more expensive choice.

699
00:21:52,750 --> 00:21:55,849
Third, they buy reliability over status.

700
00:21:56,029 --> 00:21:59,777
Models with strong reliability records and reasonable

701
00:21:59,777 --> 00:22:00,697
repair costs.

702
00:22:00,877 --> 00:22:02,928
They're not trying to impress anyone at

703
00:22:02,928 --> 00:22:03,559
a stoplight.

704
00:22:03,739 --> 00:22:05,958
And fourth, they drive the car for

705
00:22:05,958 --> 00:22:06,741
a long time,

706
00:22:06,741 --> 00:22:09,091
often until it's genuinely worn out,

707
00:22:09,091 --> 00:22:11,506
and they bank the payment free years.

708
00:22:11,686 --> 00:22:12,963
That's not being cheap.

709
00:22:13,143 --> 00:22:15,469
It's choosing where money creates the most

710
00:22:15,469 --> 00:22:15,801
value.

711
00:22:16,152 --> 00:22:17,638
And here's the honest truth.

712
00:22:17,818 --> 00:22:20,552
Plenty of wealthy people do love cars,

713
00:22:20,552 --> 00:22:22,566
and some buy expensive ones.

714
00:22:22,746 --> 00:22:24,023
The difference is the order.

715
00:22:24,203 --> 00:22:25,719
They build wealth first,

716
00:22:25,719 --> 00:22:27,614
and buy the fun car with money

717
00:22:27,614 --> 00:22:29,635
that's already working for them,

718
00:22:29,635 --> 00:22:30,519
often in cash.

719
00:22:30,699 --> 00:22:32,413
Most people do it the other way

720
00:22:32,413 --> 00:22:32,800
around.

721
00:22:32,980 --> 00:22:34,570
They buy the fun car first,

722
00:22:34,570 --> 00:22:35,276
with a loan,

723
00:22:35,276 --> 00:22:37,160
and hope the wealth comes later.

724
00:22:37,510 --> 00:22:40,041
The car trap doesn't hit everyone equally.

725
00:22:40,221 --> 00:22:43,118
Borrowers with lower credit scores often pay

726
00:22:43,118 --> 00:22:44,896
much higher interest rates,

727
00:22:44,896 --> 00:22:47,991
sometimes double or triple what prime borrowers

728
00:22:47,991 --> 00:22:48,255
pay.

729
00:22:48,435 --> 00:22:50,457
That means the people with the least

730
00:22:50,457 --> 00:22:52,423
room in their budgets often pay the

731
00:22:52,423 --> 00:22:53,659
most for the same car.

732
00:22:53,839 --> 00:22:56,078
Some buy here pay here lots charge

733
00:22:56,078 --> 00:22:58,843
very high rates and can repossess quickly.

734
00:22:59,193 --> 00:23:01,793
Young drivers are another group at risk.

735
00:23:01,973 --> 00:23:04,454
Their first car loan often comes before

736
00:23:04,454 --> 00:23:05,981
they've built a cushion,

737
00:23:05,981 --> 00:23:08,080
with high insurance costs on top.

738
00:23:08,260 --> 00:23:10,300
A big payment at twenty two can

739
00:23:10,300 --> 00:23:11,747
delay everything else,

740
00:23:11,747 --> 00:23:14,313
moving out, building an emergency fund,

741
00:23:14,313 --> 00:23:15,365
investing early.

742
00:23:15,545 --> 00:23:16,530
If you're young,

743
00:23:16,530 --> 00:23:18,682
the best car is usually a reliable,

744
00:23:18,682 --> 00:23:20,897
boring one that leaves room for your

745
00:23:20,897 --> 00:23:21,327
future.

746
00:23:21,677 --> 00:23:23,341
If you're in either group,

747
00:23:23,341 --> 00:23:25,901
the most powerful step isn't finding the

748
00:23:25,901 --> 00:23:26,669
perfect car.

749
00:23:26,849 --> 00:23:29,466
It's improving your credit before you borrow,

750
00:23:29,466 --> 00:23:30,397
if you can wait,

751
00:23:30,397 --> 00:23:32,258
and keeping the loan as small as

752
00:23:32,258 --> 00:23:32,782
possible.

753
00:23:32,962 --> 00:23:34,658
Even a few months of on time

754
00:23:34,658 --> 00:23:36,112
payments on other bills,

755
00:23:36,112 --> 00:23:37,566
and lower card balances,

756
00:23:37,566 --> 00:23:38,777
can move your score,

757
00:23:38,777 --> 00:23:39,626
and your rate,

758
00:23:39,626 --> 00:23:41,019
in the right direction.

759
00:23:41,369 --> 00:23:43,453
Here's a simple checklist for the day

760
00:23:43,453 --> 00:23:44,410
you actually buy.

761
00:23:44,590 --> 00:23:46,123
Screenshot this part.

762
00:23:46,473 --> 00:23:47,309
Before you go.

763
00:23:47,489 --> 00:23:49,473
Know your budget and the full monthly

764
00:23:49,473 --> 00:23:49,741
cost.

765
00:23:49,921 --> 00:23:51,929
Have your pre approval letter.

766
00:23:52,109 --> 00:23:54,296
Have your trade in offers from other

767
00:23:54,296 --> 00:23:54,722
buyers.

768
00:23:54,902 --> 00:23:57,368
Have the insurance quote for the exact

769
00:23:57,368 --> 00:23:57,758
model.

770
00:23:57,938 --> 00:23:59,452
And have a written out the door

771
00:23:59,452 --> 00:24:01,014
price from at least two dealers.

772
00:24:01,194 --> 00:24:02,482
If you have all five,

773
00:24:02,482 --> 00:24:04,689
you're already ahead of most buyers.

774
00:24:05,039 --> 00:24:06,083
At the dealership.

775
00:24:06,263 --> 00:24:08,284
Test drive the exact car.

776
00:24:08,464 --> 00:24:10,373
Confirm the out the door price in

777
00:24:10,373 --> 00:24:12,051
writing before anything else.

778
00:24:12,231 --> 00:24:13,671
Then discuss your trade in.

779
00:24:13,851 --> 00:24:16,358
Then financing, comparing their rate with your

780
00:24:16,358 --> 00:24:17,067
pre approval.

781
00:24:17,247 --> 00:24:19,278
Read every line of the contract,

782
00:24:19,278 --> 00:24:21,564
and make sure the numbers match what

783
00:24:21,564 --> 00:24:22,262
you agreed.

784
00:24:22,442 --> 00:24:24,908
Mistakes happen, and they rarely happen in

785
00:24:24,908 --> 00:24:25,554
your favor.

786
00:24:25,904 --> 00:24:27,250
In the finance office.

787
00:24:27,430 --> 00:24:29,299
Bring your list of add ons you

788
00:24:29,299 --> 00:24:30,109
want, if any,

789
00:24:30,109 --> 00:24:31,355
with outside prices.

790
00:24:31,535 --> 00:24:32,637
For everything else,

791
00:24:32,637 --> 00:24:34,402
a polite no thank you is enough.

792
00:24:34,582 --> 00:24:36,960
Watch for products that appear in the

793
00:24:36,960 --> 00:24:39,145
paperwork without being discussed.

794
00:24:39,325 --> 00:24:41,531
And remember, you can always leave.

795
00:24:41,711 --> 00:24:43,795
A dealer who pressures you to sign

796
00:24:43,795 --> 00:24:45,635
right now is giving you a very

797
00:24:45,635 --> 00:24:47,167
good reason to walk away.

798
00:24:47,518 --> 00:24:48,156
And after.

799
00:24:48,336 --> 00:24:49,962
Set up autopay on the loan.

800
00:24:50,142 --> 00:24:50,864
If you can,

801
00:24:50,864 --> 00:24:52,244
round the payment up,

802
00:24:52,244 --> 00:24:54,674
even by twenty five or fifty dollars,

803
00:24:54,674 --> 00:24:56,908
so the extra goes to principal and

804
00:24:56,908 --> 00:24:58,025
you finish early.

805
00:24:58,205 --> 00:25:00,817
Put your maintenance schedule in your calendar.

806
00:25:00,997 --> 00:25:02,486
And open your car fund,

807
00:25:02,486 --> 00:25:04,881
so your next car starts saving itself

808
00:25:04,881 --> 00:25:05,269
today.

809
00:25:05,620 --> 00:25:08,545
Let's compare two drivers over ten years.

810
00:25:08,725 --> 00:25:10,432
Mike loves new cars.

811
00:25:10,612 --> 00:25:12,590
Every four years or so he trades

812
00:25:12,590 --> 00:25:13,641
in for a new one,

813
00:25:13,641 --> 00:25:16,422
usually rolling a little negative equity into

814
00:25:16,422 --> 00:25:17,288
the next loan.

815
00:25:17,468 --> 00:25:20,021
His payment hovers around seven hundred fifty

816
00:25:20,021 --> 00:25:20,928
dollars a month,

817
00:25:20,928 --> 00:25:22,460
and he's never without one.

818
00:25:22,810 --> 00:25:23,709
Over ten years,

819
00:25:23,709 --> 00:25:26,227
Mike pays about ninety thousand dollars in

820
00:25:26,227 --> 00:25:28,085
car payments, and at the end he

821
00:25:28,085 --> 00:25:30,423
owns a car worth maybe thirty thousand,

822
00:25:30,423 --> 00:25:32,341
with a loan balance still on it.

823
00:25:32,521 --> 00:25:34,872
His insurance has been higher too,

824
00:25:34,872 --> 00:25:37,914
because newer, more expensive cars cost more

825
00:25:37,914 --> 00:25:38,605
to insure.

826
00:25:38,955 --> 00:25:41,209
Sarah buys a reliable three year old

827
00:25:41,209 --> 00:25:42,963
car with a four year loan at

828
00:25:42,963 --> 00:25:45,154
about four hundred dollars a month.

829
00:25:45,334 --> 00:25:46,528
When the loan ends,

830
00:25:46,528 --> 00:25:48,977
she keeps paying four hundred dollars a

831
00:25:48,977 --> 00:25:51,175
month, but into her own savings and

832
00:25:51,175 --> 00:25:51,929
investments.

833
00:25:52,109 --> 00:25:54,097
She drives the same car for ten

834
00:25:54,097 --> 00:25:56,598
years, with some bigger repair bills in

835
00:25:56,598 --> 00:25:57,624
the later years.

836
00:25:57,974 --> 00:25:59,007
Over ten years,

837
00:25:59,007 --> 00:26:02,106
Sarah pays about nineteen thousand dollars in

838
00:26:02,106 --> 00:26:03,070
loan payments.

839
00:26:03,250 --> 00:26:04,557
For the last six years,

840
00:26:04,557 --> 00:26:06,659
her four hundred dollars a month goes

841
00:26:06,659 --> 00:26:07,796
to her own accounts,

842
00:26:07,796 --> 00:26:10,353
about twenty nine thousand dollars before any

843
00:26:10,353 --> 00:26:10,750
growth.

844
00:26:10,930 --> 00:26:13,669
Even after setting aside several thousand for

845
00:26:13,669 --> 00:26:16,043
repairs, the gap between Mike and Sarah

846
00:26:16,043 --> 00:26:18,478
can easily reach fifty to sixty thousand

847
00:26:18,478 --> 00:26:18,965
dollars.

848
00:26:19,145 --> 00:26:20,143
Same decade.

849
00:26:20,323 --> 00:26:21,194
Same need.

850
00:26:21,374 --> 00:26:22,929
Very different results.

851
00:26:23,280 --> 00:26:24,383
And notice.

852
00:26:24,563 --> 00:26:26,559
Sarah didn't drive a junk car.

853
00:26:26,739 --> 00:26:27,982
She didn't suffer.

854
00:26:28,162 --> 00:26:30,467
She simply let someone else pay the

855
00:26:30,467 --> 00:26:32,245
steep part of depreciation,

856
00:26:32,245 --> 00:26:33,562
kept her car longer,

857
00:26:33,562 --> 00:26:36,196
and turned her old payment into savings.

858
00:26:36,376 --> 00:26:38,384
Mike didn't do anything crazy either.

859
00:26:38,564 --> 00:26:40,492
He just followed what felt normal.

860
00:26:40,672 --> 00:26:41,670
That's the car trap.

861
00:26:41,850 --> 00:26:43,394
Normal is expensive.

862
00:26:43,745 --> 00:26:45,846
Here's one more way to see it.

863
00:26:46,026 --> 00:26:48,121
If Sarah invests her freed up four

864
00:26:48,121 --> 00:26:50,400
hundred dollars a month for those six

865
00:26:50,400 --> 00:26:52,865
years at a seven percent average return,

866
00:26:52,865 --> 00:26:55,021
it could grow to around thirty five

867
00:26:55,021 --> 00:26:56,069
thousand dollars.

868
00:26:56,249 --> 00:26:58,044
And if she keeps going after the

869
00:26:58,044 --> 00:27:00,568
decade ends, that habit keeps compounding for

870
00:27:00,568 --> 00:27:01,747
the rest of her life,

871
00:27:01,747 --> 00:27:03,822
while Mike is still choosing his next

872
00:27:03,822 --> 00:27:04,271
payment.

873
00:27:04,451 --> 00:27:06,216
The car didn't make the difference.

874
00:27:06,396 --> 00:27:08,033
The payment free years did.

875
00:27:08,383 --> 00:27:10,204
So if you take one thing from

876
00:27:10,204 --> 00:27:11,900
this story, let it be this.

877
00:27:12,080 --> 00:27:14,366
The goal isn't to never enjoy a

878
00:27:14,366 --> 00:27:15,029
nice car.

879
00:27:15,209 --> 00:27:17,198
It's to spend fewer years of your

880
00:27:17,198 --> 00:27:18,704
life making car payments.

881
00:27:18,884 --> 00:27:20,926
Every payment free year is a raise

882
00:27:20,926 --> 00:27:22,007
you give yourself.

883
00:27:22,357 --> 00:27:24,624
Let me answer the questions people ask

884
00:27:24,624 --> 00:27:26,234
me most about car payments.

885
00:27:26,585 --> 00:27:27,571
Question one.

886
00:27:27,751 --> 00:27:29,458
Is leasing better than buying?

887
00:27:29,638 --> 00:27:31,950
Leasing can make sense for some people,

888
00:27:31,950 --> 00:27:33,669
like those who want a new car

889
00:27:33,669 --> 00:27:36,040
every few years and drive limited miles,

890
00:27:36,040 --> 00:27:37,463
or some business owners.

891
00:27:37,643 --> 00:27:40,868
But leasing usually means a permanent payment,

892
00:27:40,868 --> 00:27:43,181
and fees for extra miles or wear.

893
00:27:43,361 --> 00:27:45,927
For most people trying to build wealth,

894
00:27:45,927 --> 00:27:48,295
buying a reliable car and keeping it

895
00:27:48,295 --> 00:27:50,335
for a long time costs less over

896
00:27:50,335 --> 00:27:50,664
time.

897
00:27:51,014 --> 00:27:51,861
Question two.

898
00:27:52,041 --> 00:27:53,794
Should I pay cash for a car?

899
00:27:53,974 --> 00:27:56,176
If you can pay cash without draining

900
00:27:56,176 --> 00:27:57,399
your emergency fund,

901
00:27:57,399 --> 00:27:58,990
it's often a great choice.

902
00:27:59,170 --> 00:28:00,748
No interest, no lender,

903
00:28:00,748 --> 00:28:02,943
and no risk of being underwater.

904
00:28:03,123 --> 00:28:04,993
But don't empty your savings for a

905
00:28:04,993 --> 00:28:05,213
car.

906
00:28:05,393 --> 00:28:07,419
A car paid in cash with no

907
00:28:07,419 --> 00:28:09,445
emergency fund is fragile.

908
00:28:09,625 --> 00:28:10,752
Keep the cushion,

909
00:28:10,752 --> 00:28:11,947
then use the rest.

910
00:28:12,297 --> 00:28:13,272
Question three.

911
00:28:13,452 --> 00:28:16,261
What about zero percent financing deals?

912
00:28:16,441 --> 00:28:18,587
Sometimes they're genuinely good,

913
00:28:18,587 --> 00:28:21,318
especially on new cars from manufacturers.

914
00:28:21,498 --> 00:28:23,568
But they're often only for buyers with

915
00:28:23,568 --> 00:28:25,911
excellent credit, and sometimes you have to

916
00:28:25,911 --> 00:28:27,437
give up a cash rebate to get

917
00:28:27,437 --> 00:28:27,709
them.

918
00:28:27,889 --> 00:28:29,480
Do the math both ways.

919
00:28:29,660 --> 00:28:31,926
A rebate plus a low credit union

920
00:28:31,926 --> 00:28:34,547
rate can sometimes beat zero percent.

921
00:28:34,898 --> 00:28:35,884
Question four.

922
00:28:36,064 --> 00:28:37,794
How long should a car last?

923
00:28:37,974 --> 00:28:39,924
It depends on the model and how

924
00:28:39,924 --> 00:28:40,930
you care for it,

925
00:28:40,930 --> 00:28:43,257
but many modern cars can run reliably

926
00:28:43,257 --> 00:28:45,521
for well over ten years with regular

927
00:28:45,521 --> 00:28:46,275
maintenance.

928
00:28:46,455 --> 00:28:47,446
A useful rule:

929
00:28:47,446 --> 00:28:50,843
when annual repair costs regularly approach what

930
00:28:50,843 --> 00:28:53,320
a year of payments on a replacement

931
00:28:53,320 --> 00:28:55,867
would be, or the car becomes unsafe,

932
00:28:55,867 --> 00:28:58,344
it's time to consider replacing it.

933
00:28:58,694 --> 00:28:59,669
Question five.

934
00:28:59,849 --> 00:29:01,590
Is an electric vehicle cheaper?

935
00:29:01,770 --> 00:29:02,494
It can be,

936
00:29:02,494 --> 00:29:03,941
depending on prices,

937
00:29:03,941 --> 00:29:07,269
incentives, electricity costs and how much you

938
00:29:07,269 --> 00:29:07,703
drive.

939
00:29:07,883 --> 00:29:09,996
Fuel and maintenance can be lower.

940
00:29:10,176 --> 00:29:11,538
But purchase prices,

941
00:29:11,538 --> 00:29:14,467
insurance and depreciation can be higher on

942
00:29:14,467 --> 00:29:15,284
some models.

943
00:29:15,464 --> 00:29:17,453
Run the full cost numbers for your

944
00:29:17,453 --> 00:29:19,617
situation, the same way you would for

945
00:29:19,617 --> 00:29:20,085
any car.

946
00:29:20,436 --> 00:29:21,956
And question six.

947
00:29:22,136 --> 00:29:24,296
My payment is already too high.

948
00:29:24,476 --> 00:29:25,602
What do I do today?

949
00:29:25,782 --> 00:29:27,476
First, look at refinancing,

950
00:29:27,476 --> 00:29:30,235
especially if your credit has improved since

951
00:29:30,235 --> 00:29:30,925
you bought.

952
00:29:31,105 --> 00:29:33,596
Second, put any extra money toward the

953
00:29:33,596 --> 00:29:34,252
principal.

954
00:29:34,432 --> 00:29:37,398
Third, cut other costs temporarily to protect

955
00:29:37,398 --> 00:29:38,716
your emergency fund.

956
00:29:38,896 --> 00:29:41,203
And fourth, if it's truly unworkable,

957
00:29:41,203 --> 00:29:42,824
talk to your lender early,

958
00:29:42,824 --> 00:29:44,445
before you miss a payment.

959
00:29:44,625 --> 00:29:47,359
Lenders usually prefer a solution to a

960
00:29:47,359 --> 00:29:48,294
repossession.

961
00:29:48,644 --> 00:29:49,596
Question seven.

962
00:29:49,776 --> 00:29:51,250
I have a growing family.

963
00:29:51,430 --> 00:29:53,311
Don't I need a big new SUV?

964
00:29:53,491 --> 00:29:54,951
You might need more space,

965
00:29:54,951 --> 00:29:56,242
and that's a real need.

966
00:29:56,422 --> 00:29:58,602
But space doesn't have to mean new

967
00:29:58,602 --> 00:29:59,244
or luxury.

968
00:29:59,424 --> 00:30:01,896
A three to five year old minivan

969
00:30:01,896 --> 00:30:04,446
or midsize SUV can carry a family

970
00:30:04,446 --> 00:30:06,146
safely for a lot less.

971
00:30:06,326 --> 00:30:07,972
Safety ratings are public,

972
00:30:07,972 --> 00:30:10,378
so you can compare used models easily.

973
00:30:10,558 --> 00:30:12,088
Buy the space you need,

974
00:30:12,088 --> 00:30:13,019
not the badge.

975
00:30:13,369 --> 00:30:14,286
Question eight.

976
00:30:14,466 --> 00:30:15,940
How much should I put down?

977
00:30:16,120 --> 00:30:17,768
More is generally better,

978
00:30:17,768 --> 00:30:19,548
because it lowers the loan,

979
00:30:19,548 --> 00:30:21,986
the interest and the risk of negative

980
00:30:21,986 --> 00:30:22,448
equity.

981
00:30:22,628 --> 00:30:24,787
Twenty percent is a common target.

982
00:30:24,967 --> 00:30:26,955
If you can't put anything down,

983
00:30:26,955 --> 00:30:29,327
that's a signal to consider a cheaper

984
00:30:29,327 --> 00:30:30,930
car, or to save for a few

985
00:30:30,930 --> 00:30:32,084
more months first.

986
00:30:32,264 --> 00:30:34,330
A few months of patience can save

987
00:30:34,330 --> 00:30:35,457
years of payments.

988
00:30:35,807 --> 00:30:37,455
And one bonus question.

989
00:30:37,635 --> 00:30:39,446
What about buying a car with a

990
00:30:39,446 --> 00:30:40,050
co signer?

991
00:30:40,230 --> 00:30:42,219
Co signing means the other person is

992
00:30:42,219 --> 00:30:44,263
fully responsible for the debt if you

993
00:30:44,263 --> 00:30:44,816
don't pay.

994
00:30:44,996 --> 00:30:47,834
It can damage relationships and credit on

995
00:30:47,834 --> 00:30:48,595
both sides.

996
00:30:48,775 --> 00:30:50,319
If a car only works with a

997
00:30:50,319 --> 00:30:52,041
co signer, it might be a sign

998
00:30:52,041 --> 00:30:53,941
to choose a cheaper car for now.

999
00:30:54,292 --> 00:30:55,081
Quick recap.

1000
00:30:55,261 --> 00:30:58,194
The numbers: around one point six trillion

1001
00:30:58,194 --> 00:30:59,661
dollars in auto debt,

1002
00:30:59,661 --> 00:31:02,943
payments around seven hundred fifty dollars for

1003
00:31:02,943 --> 00:31:05,807
new cars, prices close to fifty thousand,

1004
00:31:05,807 --> 00:31:07,693
longer loans, higher rates,

1005
00:31:07,693 --> 00:31:11,045
fast depreciation, and full ownership costs over

1006
00:31:11,045 --> 00:31:13,210
eleven thousand dollars a year.

1007
00:31:13,390 --> 00:31:14,846
Plus negative equity,

1008
00:31:14,846 --> 00:31:17,620
and the hidden opportunity cost of every

1009
00:31:17,620 --> 00:31:18,522
extra dollar.

1010
00:31:18,872 --> 00:31:21,906
The psychology: the monthly payment question,

1011
00:31:21,906 --> 00:31:23,930
juggling four numbers at once,

1012
00:31:23,930 --> 00:31:25,481
finance office add ons,

1013
00:31:25,481 --> 00:31:26,627
cars as identity,

1014
00:31:26,627 --> 00:31:28,448
the fading new car feeling,

1015
00:31:28,448 --> 00:31:30,876
and thinking everyone has a payment.

1016
00:31:31,227 --> 00:31:32,622
And the playbook:

1017
00:31:32,622 --> 00:31:35,002
use the twenty four ten rule,

1018
00:31:35,002 --> 00:31:36,561
know the full cost,

1019
00:31:36,561 --> 00:31:38,038
buy slightly used,

1020
00:31:38,038 --> 00:31:39,434
get pre approved,

1021
00:31:39,434 --> 00:31:42,142
negotiate the out the door price,

1022
00:31:42,142 --> 00:31:44,440
handle trade ins separately,

1023
00:31:44,440 --> 00:31:45,753
skip the extras,

1024
00:31:45,753 --> 00:31:47,230
keep the car long,

1025
00:31:47,230 --> 00:31:49,446
pay yourself a car payment,

1026
00:31:49,446 --> 00:31:52,647
and never roll negative equity forward.

1027
00:31:52,997 --> 00:31:54,825
If you want the full plan for

1028
00:31:54,825 --> 00:31:56,402
every big money decision,

1029
00:31:56,402 --> 00:31:57,285
not just cars,

1030
00:31:57,285 --> 00:31:58,546
it's in my playbook.

1031
00:31:58,726 --> 00:32:00,317
The link is in the description.

1032
00:32:00,497 --> 00:32:02,388
And watch my video on how credit

1033
00:32:02,388 --> 00:32:04,456
card companies make money from you.

1034
00:32:04,636 --> 00:32:06,369
The same tricks show up in a

1035
00:32:06,369 --> 00:32:07,236
lot of places.

1036
00:32:07,416 --> 00:32:08,740
I'm Ryan Rover.

1037
00:32:08,920 --> 00:32:10,069
See you in the next one.
